// "before adding a new body, ask whether it's one of the eight things above; if not, it should be a method
// on some Interface". Re-arm isn't any of establishing a connection, discovering an endpoint, making a call,
// returning a result, cancelling, subscribing, pushing events or keeping the connection alive.
//
// # Mechanism in the kernel, policy in the service
//
// On Rearm the kernel only re-checks one **hard precondition that can't be bypassed**: the stop progress must have settled,
// either the normal chain reached OUTPUT_DISABLED / STANDSTILL_CONFIRMED (output confirmed off)
// or it entered a terminal side branch such as DELIVERY_FAULT / STANDSTILL_TIMEOUT that needs human handling.
// Releasing the latch while the stop is still in progress would allow motion again without knowing whether the output is off.
//
// "Should we re-arm right now" is itself policy and isn't in the kernel: a system service (nervus.safety.recovery)
// decides after aggregating device state, risk and user policy, then calls this method.
//
// # What this interface can't do
//
// **There's no Trip method.** Software emergency stops are triggered by other paths (a Vital component's circuit breaking, a deadman timeout,
// a Provider reporting a fault); this interface only observes and recovers. Making "stop the machine" an
// RPC any App can call would turn one mistaken call into a production incident, and the emergency stop should be backed by
// physical buttons and the kernel's supervision chain, not depend on a network round trip.
